<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=parchment">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Writing and counting</p><h1 class="enc-title">Parchment</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 2350 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/24th-century-bce">24th century BCE</a> · Also known as vellum</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Parchment is a writing material made from specially prepared untanned skins of animals, primarily sheep, calves and goats; the finer form made from calfskin is called vellum. Writing on leather is recorded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> from the Fourth Dynasty, c. 2550–2450 BCE, and a surviving fragmentary roll dates to the Sixth Dynasty, in the 24th century BCE. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonians">Babylonians</a> wrote on parchment from the 6th century BCE. The word derives from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pergamon">Pergamon</a>, a centre of production in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellenistic-period">Hellenistic period</a>. By 400 CE many works meant for preservation had passed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papyrus">papyrus</a> to parchment; in the 15th century paper largely replaced it, though it is still used for ritual and legal purposes.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/parchment.jpg" alt="Parchment" width="330" height="174" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: This illustration was made by (Royonx) and released under the license(s) stated above. You are free to use it for any purpose as long · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1532-08-20_Cochony_Not_4.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Land documents and letters on <b>parchment</b> and paper from Qasr Ibrim show a society of landholders, churches and monasteries that bought and sold fields, mostly without coinage.</blockquote><a href="/empire/makuria">Makuria · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Fragments of the Quran on <b>parchment</b> from the 7th century, such as the Birmingham manuscript, confirm that its text was fixed early.</blockquote><a href="/religion/islam">Islam · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>In the 1960s a tiny Greek <b>parchment</b> book of the 5th century, the Cologne Mani Codex, was identified, containing an account of Mani’s early life.</blockquote><a href="/religion/manichaeism">Manichaeism · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>…Phoenicians are believed to have written much more, but their script was easy to write on papyrus or <b>parchment</b>, and in the damp air and soil of their coastal cities these rotted away: virtually no Phoenician history or trading records survive, and no Tyrian sources from the time…</blockquote><a href="/language/phoenician">Phoenician · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>Books on papyrus and <b>parchment</b> have perished, among them the annals of Tyre that Josephus quoted, leaving inscriptions on stone and metal, most of them short dedications and epitaphs; the royal texts of Byblos and Sidon and the bilingual inscriptions of Karatepe in Anatolia and Pyrgi in Italy…</blockquote><a href="/culture/phoenicians">Phoenicians · Arts &amp; Knowledge</a></li><li><blockquote>The poem survives in a single manuscript in ink on <b>parchment</b>, the Nowell Codex, named after the 16th-century scholar Laurence Nowell; Kevin Kiernan argues that Nowell most likely acquired it through William Cecil in 1563.</blockquote><a href="/work/beowulf">Beowulf · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>c. 2350 BCE</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Egypt</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Pliny the Elder's account that parchment was invented at Pergamon to replace papyrus is almost assuredly false, since parchment was in use in Anatolia and elsewhere long before Pergamon. A first mention of Egyptian leather documents goes back to the Fourth Dynasty (c. 2550–2450 BCE).</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Led to</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/codex">Codex<span>1st century CE</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=parchment">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
